<p>In all three of these figures, the gray ring&#8217;s color is uniform. But in the second and third figures the upper half appears distinctly darker, showing that the brain exaggerates differences in brightness between adjacent surfaces. German psychologist Kurt Koffka first reported the effect in 1935.</p>
